From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: DCDC4 is VDD-GPU, and it gets a window because the GPU is pinned

The GPU rail has had no device-tree node. That was correct while the GPU ran
at the operating point the boot chain leaves behind -- 600 MHz off PERI0_600M
at whatever DCDC4 was already set to, measured 800 mV. It stops being correct
the moment the next patch pins the core to 1008 MHz, because 1008 MHz is the
row of the vendor OPP table that reads 960 mV.

The rule this repo applies to PMIC rails is that a rail gets a node -- and
therefore a window, and therefore the right to be written -- only together with
its consumer (the DCDC3/DCDC5 nodes above say the same in
their own comment). The consumer here is not a driver: drm/powervr reads no
regulator at all. Grepped in the tree we build rather than assumed -- zero hits
for "regulator" and for "-supply" across drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/. The
consumer is the pinned clock, which is why these two patches are one change.

⚠ SPLIT THEM AND ONE HALF IS A HAZARD. This rail alone at 960 mV is merely
wasteful. The clock alone at 1008 MHz runs the GPU under its voltage curve,
which is the failure mode that does not announce itself.

The ordering is right by construction and not by luck: the regulator core
writes this rail when the PMIC probes, and the GPU's rate is applied when
powervr probes, seconds later. Voltage up first, then clock.

min == max is deliberate. This is a FIXED operating point, not a ladder: there
is no OPP table, no devfreq, and no consumer that could ever narrow a wider
window. A window here is enforcement, not a wish.

SILICON (the 6 GiB board, i2cget -f -y 1 0x36; 0x15/0x1b first read 2026-07-22, re-read
2026-09-08 and identical both times):

  0x15 = 0x9e   DVM on, n = 30 -> 500 + 30*10 = 800 mV, which is exactly what
                the regulator framework reports for this rail -- register
                decode and sysfs agree, so the coding is confirmed rather than
                assumed
  0x1b = 0x00   poly-phase bits [6] and [7] both clear, so DCDC2+3 and DCDC4+5
                are NOT paralleled. This is the load-bearing read: it is what
                makes DCDC4 the GPU's rail alone, and therefore what makes
                raising it unable to touch VDD-CPUL on DCDC5.

WINDOWS. The schematic rates VDD-GPU at 0.8-0.96 V and DS Tab. 5-1 gives
-0.3..1.2 V absolute, so 960 mV is the top of the design window and stays
240 mV under the limit.

⚠ AND 960 mV IS NOT DERIVED FROM ANY DOCUMENT -- said plainly, because the
rest of this header is derivation and this line is not. No datasheet carries an
OPP table for this SoC; freq<->voltage comes from
DT/eFuse and is silicon-bin-dependent. The two device trees that do carry a
ladder disagree with each other everywhere except this one pair (vendor:
400/600/800/1008 at 800/800/840/960; an Armbian tree: 252/504/1008 at
800/800/960). That disagreement is itself the evidence that these tables are
opinion. 960 mV is adopted because it is the conservative end of the
disagreement and inside the documented window.

It is owed a measurement, and the instrument exists: a GPU render test
fails on wrong PIXELS before a GPU hangs, so walking this rail down at 1008 MHz
until the test starts failing measures the real margin on our own silicon instead of
inheriting someone's table.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
